namespace jo

//[ The contract for the program entry point
  !
  ! The compiler detects the entry point by name and type: the one top-level
  ! parameterless function named `main` that returns `Unit`. Detection
  ! ignores the receives clause. It is an error if no such function exists.
  ! If several do, name the entry point explicitly by passing
  ! `--link jo.main=app.main` to the compiler.
  !
  ! The detected function is then linked to this declaration, and the link
  ! check requires that it receive only the context parameters declared here
  ! and those that a build binds with `--provide`. The `receives` clause lists
  ! the capabilities the runtime makes available to the entry point. It is not
  ! a requirement: a `main` that uses only some of them — or none at all —
  ! still conforms. See the Entry Point section of the language documentation.
  !
  ! There is no standard input capability. A program that reads input obtains it
  ! from a backend-specific API, such as `py.input`, and passes it on as its own
  ! context parameter.
  !
  ! The `pass` body is only a placeholder, so that this declaration is a
  ! well-formed deferred function; the program supplies the real entry point.
//]
defer def main: Unit receives IO.stdout, IO.stderr, IO.args = pass
